WEBVTT

NOTE Generated by scripts/make-captions.mjs from apps/demo-video/src/narration.ts
NOTE and the rendered narration clips. Do not edit by hand; re-run the script.

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.494
This is AeroScribe, an open prototype of an autonomous aerospace research pipeline:

2
00:00:05.514 --> 00:00:08.503
nine gated stages and eleven human checkpoints,

3
00:00:08.523 --> 00:00:11.640
from a literature search to a submission package.

4
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.704
This video follows one real run, including the parts that went wrong.

5
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:18.853
It starts with one sentence in the manuscript.

6
00:00:18.873 --> 00:00:22.133
The extrapolated drag coefficient is 0.4264,

7
00:00:22.153 --> 00:00:26.753
give or take three point three six times ten to the minus six.

8
00:00:26.773 --> 00:00:28.793
That number was not typed.

9
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:32.909
It comes from evidence record four, a CFD study over three meshes,

10
00:00:32.929 --> 00:00:36.545
and the record is sealed with a digest of its own payload.

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.280
The pipeline is nine stages and eleven checkpoints.

12
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.892
Nothing advances on its own: each stage writes its evidence,

13
00:00:43.912 --> 00:00:46.120
opens a gate, and waits for a person.

14
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.628
The CFD stage alone holds three.

15
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.687
Every stage appends exactly one record,

16
00:00:51.707 --> 00:00:54.447
and every record commits to the one before it.

17
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.907
That makes the registry a hash chain, eleven links long in this run.

18
00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:01.295
Re-walking it re-hashes every payload,

19
00:01:01.315 --> 00:01:05.087
and the API refuses a record that does not extend the chain.

20
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:10.723
Before it writes a word, stage five writes a plan: one paragraph per job,

21
00:01:10.743 --> 00:01:14.153
and the numbers each paragraph is allowed to print.

22
00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:17.313
Stage six then compares the text against the plan.

23
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:21.625
In this run it caught a paragraph printing a number the plan never declared.

24
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:25.003
Citations are set from structured records,

25
00:01:25.023 --> 00:01:27.787
then read back to prove nothing was dropped.

26
00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:32.644
This reference carries no issue number, so the formatter omits it and says so,

27
00:01:32.664 --> 00:01:35.591
rather than inventing a volume that reads well.

28
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:40.429
The figure is text you can diff: two LaTeX plot panels and a data file,

29
00:01:40.449 --> 00:01:42.573
written straight from the grid study.

30
00:01:42.593 --> 00:01:44.393
No screenshot of a plot.

31
00:01:44.413 --> 00:01:47.541
A reviewer reads the numbers LaTeX will draw.

32
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:52.107
Then the review council: seven checks with stated rule ids.

33
00:01:52.127 --> 00:01:56.623
This run came back revise, with four major findings,

34
00:01:56.643 --> 00:02:01.487
and stage eight recorded four blockers in its own words.

35
00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:02.440
The manuscript is real.

36
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.467
It is just not submittable.

37
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.600
Three things this does not claim.

38
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.640
The CFD numbers come from a synthetic solver: no Navier-Stokes equation was solved.

39
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:17.380
There is LaTeX source and no PDF, because no typesetting engine was configured.

40
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.280
And the published dashboard renders fixtures, not a live API.

41
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:26.793
AeroScribe is MIT licensed, and the site and the source are linked below.

42
00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:30.463
Every number you just heard was read from one recorded run:

43
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.841
the same capture the dashboard's tests replay against.
